{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789222574,"nonce":"lDkJZqP3DEfri11/aIiaekFVOFs=","title":"Debug interpreter aborts on the Haxe unit suite: misaligned i32 store in write_value_at","message":"Reproduce with a debug build of ash at d3d6158 (also at c2b5183):\n\n    cd ~/.cache/ash-haxe-conformance/haxe-tests-4.3.6/tests/unit\n    target/debug/ash --mode interp bin/unit.hl\n\nAbout 2.5 s in:\n\n    thread 'main' panicked at crates/ash_interp/src/interpreter/natives.rs:1487:21:\n    misaligned pointer dereference: address must be a multiple of 0x4 but is 0x3ff6e8b55\n    thread caused non-unwinding panic. aborting.\n\nLine 1487 is the HI32 arm of write_value_at: `*(addr as *mut i32) = ...`\nwith an odd address. The store goes through a raw pointer, so this is the\ndebug-only alignment check (-C debug-assertions); a release build performs\nthe unaligned store and passes, which is why CI's conformance job does not\nsee it. --mode hybrid passes the whole suite (12065 assertions) under the\nsame debug binary, so the address comes from an interpreter-only path\ncomputing a field or array slot offset that is not 4-aligned, or from\nstoring an i32 into a slot typed as something narrower.\n\nFound while gating branch caribou-heap: identical on the branch and on\nmain, so not related to the heap move. Fix is either a write_unaligned in\nthe marshaling arms (if the odd address is legitimate, e.g. a packed\nstruct) or the offset computation that produced it.","files":null}]}